problems.problem_text:
Let $n$ be an integer greater than $1$. For a positive integer $m$, let $S_{m}= \{ 1,2,\ldots, mn\}$. Suppose that there exists a $2n$-element set $T$ such that
(a) each element of $T$ is an $m$-element subset of $S_{m}$;
(b) each pair of elements of $T$ shares at most one common element;
and
(c) each element of $S_{m}$ is contained in exactly two elements of $T$.

Determine the maximum possible value of $m$ in terms of $n$.

For which positive integers $m$ does there exist an infinite arithmetic sequence of integers $a_1, a_2, . . .$ and an infinite geometric sequence of integers $g_1, g_2, . . .$ satisfying the following properties?
\begin{enumerate}
\item  $a_n - g_n$ is divisible by $m$ for all integers $n \ge 1$;
\item  $a_2 - a_1$ is not divisible by $m$.
\end{enumerate}

For each positive integer $n$, find the number of $n$-digit positive integers that satisfy both of the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item  no two consecutive digits are equal, and
\item  the last digit is a prime.
\end{enumerate}

For each integer $a_0 > 1$, define the sequence $a_0, a_1, a_2, \ldots$ for $n \geq 0$ as
$$a_{n+1} = 
\begin{cases}
\sqrt{a_n} & \text{if } \sqrt{a_n} \text{ is an integer,} \\
a_n + 3 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$
Determine all values of $a_0$ such that there exists a number $A$ such that $a_n = A$ for infinitely many values of $n$.

Find all integers $n$ for which each cell of $n \times n$ table can be filled with one of the letters $I,M$ and $O$ in such a way that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item  in each row and each column, one third of the entries are $I$, one third are $M$ and one third are $O$; and
\item in any diagonal, if the number of entries on the diagonal is a multiple of three, then one third of the entries are $I$, one third are $M$ and one third are $O$.
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Note.} The rows and columns of an $n \times n$ table are each labelled $1$ to $n$ in a natural order. Thus each cell corresponds to a pair of positive integer $(i,j)$ with $1 \le i,j \le n$. For $n>1$, the table has $4n-2$ diagonals of two types. A diagonal of first type consists all cells $(i,j)$  for which $i+j$ is a constant, and the diagonal of this second type consists all cells $(i,j)$ for which $i-j$ is constant.

Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{Q} \rightarrow \mathbb{Z} $ satisfying 
\[ f \left( \frac{f(x)+a} {b}\right) = f \left( \frac{x+a}{b} \right) \]
for all  $x \in \mathbb{Q}$, $a \in \mathbb{Z}$, and $b \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$. (Here, $\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ denotes the set of positive integers.)

In each square of a garden shaped like a $2022 \times 2022$ board, there is initially a tree of height $0$. A gardener and a lumberjack alternate turns playing the following game, with the gardener taking the first turn:
\begin{enumerate}
\item  The gardener chooses a square in the garden. Each tree on that square and all the surrounding squares (of which there are at most eight) then becomes one unit taller.
\item  The lumberjack then chooses four different squares on the board. Each tree of positive height on those squares then becomes one unit shorter.
\end{enumerate}
We say that a tree is \textit{majestic} if its height is at least $10^6$. Determine the largest $K$ such that the gardener can ensure there are eventually $K$ majestic trees on the board, no matter how the lumberjack plays.

